subproblems.natural_proof:
This accepted declaration is reused verbatim (byte-identical frozen type). The proof below proves its exact statement. Source: parent proof, step 0(c), (S1): if F(ω) = 0 for every ω whose tsupport is disjoint from a closed set, then spt F lies in that set. Here the set is a compact K ⊆ U with U open.

1. Points outside K. Let y ∈ U ∖ K. Since U is open and K is closed, U ∖ K is open, so there is s > 0 with B_s(y) ⊆ U ∖ K. Put W := B_{s/2}(y). Then:
   (a) W is open and y ∈ W;
   (b) closure W ⊆ B̄_{s/2}(y) ⊆ B_s(y) ⊆ U ∖ K ⊆ U;
   (c) closure W is compact, being a closed subset of the compact ball B̄_{s/2}(y);
   (d) if ω ∈ 𝒟ⁿ(U) and tsupport ω ⊆ W, then tsupport ω is disjoint from K, so F(ω) = 0.
   By (a)–(d), W is one of the sets whose union is the zero region Z(F). So y ∈ Z(F) and y ∉ spt F.
2. Inclusion. spt F = U ∖ Z(F) ⊆ U, and by step 1 no point of U ∖ K is in spt F. Hence spt F ⊆ K.
3. Compactness. Since K ⊆ U, spt F = K ∖ Z(F). Z(F) is open as a union of open sets, so spt F is a closed subset of the compact K, hence compact (IsCompact.diff). ∎
